-- title:
--   Lemma 4.1 — the partial sparse-simplex step decreases f by (4.6) when ‖xᵏ‖₀ < s and by A(xᵏ) (4.7)–(4.8) when ‖xᵏ‖₀ = s
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ be continuously differentiable and bounded below, with Lipschitz gradient (Assumption 2), let $0<s<n$, and let $L_2>0$ satisfy the local Lipschitz condition (2.15). Let $(x^k)$ be a sequence generated by the partial sparse-simplex method. Then:
--
--   1. for every $k$ with $\|x^k\|_0<s$,
--   $$f(x^k)-f(x^{k+1})\ \ge\ \frac{1}{2L_2}\max_{i=1,\dots,n}\big(\nabla_if(x^k)\big)^2; \tag{4.6}$$
--   2. for every $k$ with $\|x^k\|_0=s$,
--   $$f(x^k)-f(x^{k+1})\ \ge\ A(x^k), \tag{4.7}$$
--   where
--   $$A(x)=\max\Big\{\frac1{2L_2}\max_{i\in I_1(x)}(\nabla_if(x))^2,\ M_s(x)\Big[\max_{i\in I_0(x)}|\nabla_if(x)|-\max_{i\in I_1(x)}|\nabla_if(x)|-L_2M_s(x)\Big]\Big\}. \tag{4.8}$$
--
--   The lemma converts each step of the method into a quantitative decrease of the objective. Since $f$ is bounded below, the decreases are summable, which forces the right-hand sides to vanish along convergent subsequences; this is how Theorem 3.4 is obtained.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The page's lemma speaks of "the sparse-simplex method"; in §4.2.3 this is the partial sparse-simplex method. A STOP of the method is modelled as $x^{k+1}=x^k$, so (4.6) then says $\nabla f(x^k)=0$. The maxima are suprema in $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, which agree with the maxima on the nonempty index sets involved. Assumptions 1 and 2 are kept as standing hypotheses.
-- source:
--   Beck, Eldar, Sparsity Constrained Nonlinear Optimization: Optimality Conditions and Algorithms, SIAM J. Optim. (2013), doi:10.1137/120869778 (source: arXiv:1203.4580v1), p. 30, Lemma 4.1 (4.6)–(4.8)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SparseNLO_Partial_Method

namespace SparseNLO.Partial

/-- Lemma 4.1 (p. 30): under Assumption 2, with `L2 > 0` satisfying (2.15), the sequence
generated by the partial sparse-simplex method satisfies, for every `k` with `‖xᵏ‖₀ < s`,
`f(xᵏ) − f(xᵏ⁺¹) ≥ (1/(2 L2)) max_{i=1,…,n} (∇ᵢf(xᵏ))²` (4.6), and for every `k` with
`‖xᵏ‖₀ = s`, `f(xᵏ) − f(xᵏ⁺¹) ≥ A(xᵏ)` (4.7), with `A` as in (4.8). -/
theorem lemma_4_1 {n s : ℕ} (hs : 0 < s) (hsn : s < n)
    (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (hf : ContDiff ℝ 1 f) (hbdd : ∃ γ : ℝ, ∀ x, γ ≤ f x)
    (Lf : NNReal) (hLf : LipschitzWith Lf (gradient f))
    (L2 : ℝ) (hL2pos : 0 < L2) (hL2 : LocalLipschitz f L2)
    (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hrun : IsPartialRun f s x) :
    (∀ k : ℕ, l0 (x k) < s →
      1 / (2 * L2) * maxSq Finset.univ (gradient f (x k)) ≤ f (x k) - f (x (k + 1))) ∧
    (∀ k : ℕ, l0 (x k) = s → Aval f s L2 (x k) ≤ f (x k) - f (x (k + 1))) := by sorry

end SparseNLO.Partial
